age": {"code": "ta", "name": "Tamil"}, "word": "-உள்"}, {"language": {"code": "ta", "name": "Tamil"}, "word": "முன்"}, {"language": {"code": "ta", "name": "Tamil"}, "word": "முன்பு"}], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/bɪˈfɔː/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/bɪˈfoɹ/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/biˈfoɹ/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/bəˈfoɹ/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/bɪˈfoː/", "tags": ["General Australian"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/bəˈfoː/", "tags": ["New Zealand"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/bɪˈfo(ː)ɹ/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/bɪˈfoə/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "befores", "tags": ["plural"]}, {"word": "befo", "tags": ["alternative"]}, {"word": "befo'", "tags": ["alternative"]}, {"word": "b4", "tags": ["alternative", "Internet"]}, {"word": "be4", "tags": ["alternative", "Internet"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Of before-and-after images: the one that shows the difference before a specified treatment.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "On the left of the double column of photographs are the “befores.” Look at them! Fine boys all, yet here is an unformed mouth; there, a fine face just missing strength by a slight over-plumpness of feature; below, a clever face just a shade too “smart”; on the next page, a form too slight and beginning to stoop. On the right are the “afters.”", "reference": "1918 April, “Before and After”, in Arthur Deerin Call, editor, Advocate of Peace, volume LXXX, number 4, Washington, D.C.: American Peace Society, →ISSN, →OCLC, “Editorial Notes” section, page 105, column 2:"}, {"text": "I told them that I was taking part in a ‘Before and After’ feature for the magazine. The beauty editor had picked a couple of girls in the office to experiment on. ‘I always think that the befores look better than the afters in that kind of experiment,’ Aunt G said.", "reference": "1973, Mary Lutyens, chapter 8, in Cleo, London: Michael Joseph, →ISBN, page 164:"}, {"text": "Sometimes we’d agree the person looked better in their before. Happier somehow. More themselves before the muscles. […] Other times we’d laugh at the befores. I’d make a crack, something like, “People should need a special government ID to buy spandex like that. It’s a question of public safety.”", "reference": "2017, Meagan Cass, “Calling All Soloflex Men”, in ActivAmerica, Denton, Tex.: University of North Texas Press, →ISBN, page 100:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(uncommon, often poetic) That which occurred or existed previously.", "tags": ["often", "poetic", "uncommon"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "As all are commanded to yield like the mummy when the dung beetle rolls the sun / before all the befores of the trillion nights past night and day / though I knew that the broken receding mouth of the Sphinx had nothing to add / of resurrection in the history of its grimace.", "reference": "1982, Hugh Seidman, “Hymn”, in Throne/Falcon/Eye: Poems, New York, N.Y.: Random House, →ISBN; quoted in Michael Heller, “SEIDMAN, Hugh”, in James Vinson, D[aniel] L[ane] Kirkpatrick, editors, Contemporary Poets (Contemporary Writers of the English Language), 4th edition, New York, N.Y.: St. Martin’s Press, 1985, →ISBN, pages 763–764:"}, {"text": "Your music has lasted since the beginning of the world. A stone was born in the waters. […] Voice rising to heaven, pure music, green primal root, mother-sea, before all the befores.", "reference": "1995, Maria Luisa Spaziani, translated by Laura Stortoni, “The Aegean”, in Sentry Towers, Berkeley, Calif.: Hesperia Press, →ISBN, page 41:"}, {"text": "Yet when I was a child, I stood with my mother on that distant shore, or walked with her up the road, past orchards of plums and pears, past fields narrow as piano keys. To the village where she was born, and her mother before her; all other mothers, all the befores that ever were.", "reference": "1998, Janice Kulyk Keefer, “Prologue: A Bridge of Words”, in Honey and Ashes: A Story of Family, Toronto, Ont.: HarperPerennialCanada, →ISBN, page 3:"}, {"text": "Because we’re in debt to that before, to all the befores where the life was before the words. Every time we pick up a hammer and a nail there’s a dead man in it who built a lean-to out of the weather in Wyoming in December or a farmer in Latin America someplace on the back of a mule-drawn cart broken down with a town’s vegetables rotting in the sun or a Mennonite right over there in McGaheysville this minute saying no to some nigh useless particle board siding and still fashioning his house with his hand.", "reference": "2000, Daniel Villasenor, chapter 6, in The Lake, New York, N.Y.: Viking, →ISBN, page 30:"}, {"text": "For high in the brightest tower of Light Castle, one thing shone still brighter—the Princess of Light! She came from before all the befores, from a place that is no place. Not fire. Not water. Not air. Not earth. No place. Neither solid, nor liquid, nor gas. Nothing to speak of.", "reference": "2001, Ezra Scott, “Anavak’s Tale”, in Angel Up My Sleeve, Santa Fe, N.M.: Fontanel Books, →ISBN, page 25:"}, {"text": "The packing that took all week to finish had also taken her mind off Mike. Less and less she found herself weeping. Her focus was on the nexts. There was little time now to think about all the befores. Today would put an end to the last leftover one.", "reference": "2003, Cynn Chadwick, chapter 16, in Cat Rising, Binghamton, N.Y.: Alice Street Editions, →ISBN, page 236:"}, {"text": "We talk about all the befores. The stupid things we did. The funny things. The times she made us laugh.", "reference": "2011, Mary E. Pearson, chapter 74, in The Fox Inheritance (Jenna Fox Chronicles; 2), New York, N.Y.: Henry Holt and Company, →ISBN, page 285:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(rare) A previous form or instance.", "tags": ["rare"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "A big wind blew all their befores away. Impacted teeth grew over their names. Even the lines in their hands unraveled, these are the lines they stand in to ask for their hands back.", "reference": "1999 March, Julia Vinograd, “The Homeless”, in Street Spirit, volume 5, number 3, San Francisco, Calif.: American Friends Service Committee, →OCLC, page 13:"}, {"text": "I guess I should have known from all the befores / that when it’s all said and done / I don’t want to be / I won’t stand to be / I refuse to be / anything but YOURS", "reference": "2006, Walee [pseudonym], “I Never Can Say Goodbye”, in What’s on the Menu? All of Me! Literary Entrees Prepared by Walee, Lincoln, Neb.: iUniverse, →ISBN, chapter 3, page 55:"}, {"text": "They were chatting and laughing and it made my heart hurt. They were getting out. Returning to whatever their befores were. They didn’t even glance back at the house as they climbed into the waiting vehicles.", "reference": "2015, Sarah Pinborough, chapter 14, in The Death House, London: Gollancz, →ISBN, page 160:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/before", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}